\section{Recognising FBF assignments}

Canvas gives an external-tool assignment an [[external_tool_tag_attributes]]
dictionary whose [[url]] is the launch URL.
We take the assignment to be FBF's if that URL is on an FBF host and has a
[[code]] parameter.
Matching the host keeps us from picking up another tool that happens to use
a [[code]] parameter.

<<imports>>=
import urllib.parse
<<functions>>=
def placement_code(assignment):
    """Return the FBF placement code of a Canvas `assignment`, or `None`."""
    attributes = getattr(assignment, "external_tool_tag_attributes", None)
    url = (attributes or {}).get("url") or ""
    parts = urllib.parse.urlsplit(url)
    host = parts.hostname or ""
    if not (host == "feedbackfruits.com" or host.endswith(".feedbackfruits.com")):
        return None
    return (urllib.parse.parse_qs(parts.query).get("code") or [None])[0]
@

Canvas assignments are [[canvasapi]] objects, but only the attribute matters
here, so a namespace stands in for one.

<<test functions>>=
from types import SimpleNamespace

def assignment(url):
  return SimpleNamespace(external_tool_tag_attributes={"url": url})

def test_placement_code():
  assert placement_code(assignment(
    "https://api.feedbackfruits.com/v1/lti/launch?code=e8f5")) == "e8f5"
  assert placement_code(assignment(
    "https://other.example.com/launch?code=e8f5")) is None
  assert placement_code(SimpleNamespace()) is None
