\section{One launch for all}

We launch with the first FBF assignment among those selected; any would do,
since the token is the user's.
The client gets a [[relaunch]] that repeats the same launch, for when the
tokens can no longer be refreshed in a long run.

<<imports>>=
import functools
from fbf.api import FBF
from fbf.launch import launch
<<functions>>=
def connect(canvas_url, canvas_token, assignments):
    """Launch FBF through one of the FBF `assignments`; return an `FBF`.

    Returns `None` if none of `assignments` is an FBF assignment.
    """
    for assignment in assignments:
        if placement_code(assignment):
            relaunch = functools.partial(
                launch, canvas_url, canvas_token,
                assignment.course_id, assignment.id)
            return FBF(relaunch(), relaunch=relaunch)
    return None
@


\section{Finding the activity}

[[/v1/lti/placements]] accepts [[filter[code]]], and the placement's
[[placeable]] relationship is the activity.
A word of caution from experience: FBF ignores filters it does not support
and then returns \emph{everything}---[[/v1/activities?filter[code]=...]]
returns all activities.
So we do not trust the filter but check the code of what comes back.
Should FBF one day ignore this filter too, we get the right answer more
slowly instead of a wrong one.

<<imports>>=
from fbf.schema import attribute, related_id, requires
<<required names>>=
requires("lti/placement", attributes=["code"], relationships=["placeable"])
<<functions>>=
def activity_id(fbf, code):
    """Return the id of the FBF activity placed with `code`, or `None`."""
    placements = fbf.get("v1/lti/placements", **{"filter[code]": code})
    for placement in placements.resources:
        if attribute(placement, "code") == code:
            return related_id(placement, "placeable")
    return None
@
